@meta
id: 27-10
title: Who set that
chapter: 27
type: lesson
skills: verbose-set, sub-basic
par: 48
minutes: 7
vscode: nothing — no setting of VS Code's changes what a replace-all means
open: refund.mapper.ts
vimrc: source config.vim

@brief
Rename every `orderId` in `refund.mapper.ts` to `refundId`. Four
occurrences, three lines, one substitute. You have been doing this since
chapter 14.

    :%s/orderId/refundId/g

Run it and two of the four are still `orderId`.

Nothing is wrong with your command. Something in this session has changed
what the `g` flag means, and the single most useful debugging command in
vim will tell you what, and which line of which file did it.

The file that did it is `config.vim`, sitting in this directory. It is
open to nobody and it is not yours to edit — find the line, understand it,
and get the rename done in this session.

@keys
| `:verbose set gdefault?` | the value, and the file and line that set it |
| `:verbose set` | prefix any command to make it name its source |
| `:set gdefault?` | the value alone |
| `:scriptnames` | every file vim sourced, numbered |
| `:verbose map` `:verbose au` | the same trick for mappings and autocommands |

@hints
Run the substitute and look at what survived. It is not random: it is the
first match on each line that changed, and the rest that did not. That is
the behaviour of a substitute *without* `g` — so your `g` is being read
backwards.
--
The option is `gdefault`. `:verbose set gdefault?` tells you both what it
is and who is responsible.
--
`:set nogdefault` `Enter` puts the flag back to meaning what you think it
means, and then your original substitute works as written.

@solution
    :set nogdefault<Enter>  :%s/orderId/refundId/g<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Forty-three keystrokes.

`gdefault` makes every `:s` behave as if `g` were already there — and
makes an explicit `g` *turn it off*. It was a popular setting for about
five years, on the theory that you almost always want every match. Then
people noticed that it silently changes the meaning of every substitute
they had ever learned, including the ones in every answer on the internet,
and vim's own documentation now says not to use it.

The lesson is not the option. It is the command that found it.

    :verbose set gdefault?

`:verbose` prefixed to anything makes vim name the file and line that last
set what you are asking about. It works on options, on mappings
(`:verbose nmap <C-p>`), on autocommands (`:verbose au BufWritePre`) and
on functions. When your vim does something you did not ask for — an
indent that is wrong, a key that does nothing, a file that reformats
itself on save — this is the first thing you type. It turns "one of my
plugins is doing this" into a file name and a line number in about four
seconds.

`:verbose` takes a count: `:5verbose set sw?` raises the verbosity level
and shows you more of what vim was doing at the time. You will rarely need
it. `:scriptnames` is its companion — the full list of everything vim has
sourced, in order, which is how you find out that a plugin you forgot you
installed is loading at all.

@start config.vim
" team defaults, inherited from the old tech lead
set ignorecase
set smartcase
set gdefault
set shiftwidth=2

@start refund.mapper.ts
export const toRefundRow = (orderId: string, amount: number) => ({
  order: orderId, reference: `refund-${orderId}`,
  key: `${orderId}:${amount}`, source: orderId,
});

@expected refund.mapper.ts
export const toRefundRow = (refundId: string, amount: number) => ({
  order: refundId, reference: `refund-${refundId}`,
  key: `${refundId}:${amount}`, source: refundId,
});
